Package: mos-podman
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: mos-system (= @SYSTEM_VERSION@), nftables, libsystemd0, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: mos container engine
 The seven binaries pkgs/podman compiles from pinned upstream source --
 podman and quadlet from containers/podman v5.8.6, crun 1.29.1, conmon v2.2.1,
 netavark v2.1.0, aardvark-dns v2.1.0 and catatonit v0.2.1 -- with the Quadlet
 system generator symlink, the /etc/containers configuration podman actually
 reads, and the Quadlet unit directory the operator writes into.
 .
 /usr/bin/docker IS A SYMLINK TO podman AND IS THE WHOLE OF THE DOCKER
 COMPATIBILITY THIS PACKAGE OFFERS. podman reads argv[0] to name itself and for
 nothing else -- `docker --version` prints "docker version 5.8.6" and the usage
 text says docker -- while every verb, flag and behaviour behind the name stays
 podman's. Everything Docker keeps behind its socket is absent: there is no
 daemon, no /run/docker.sock and no REST API in this image, so a client that
 dials the socket instead of exec'ing the binary gains nothing, and
 `docker compose` fails exactly as `podman compose` does -- "looking up compose
 provider failed" -- because podman shells out to a compose provider and neither
 docker-compose nor podman-compose is payload here. Quadlet is this image's
 composition mechanism.
 .
 The versions are this repository's decision and not the distribution's: trixie
 ships podman 5.4.2, crun 1.21 and netavark 1.14, and pkgs/podman/versions.env
 is the upgrade interface for all six.
 .
 THE ENGINE SHIPS INSTALLED AND INERT. This payload carries no
 multi-user.target.wants symlink, because podman ships no service units at all:
 upstream's live in contrib/ and are installed by `make install.systemd`, which
 pkgs/podman does not run. The one unit here,
 etc-containers-systemd.mount, is installed and deliberately not enabled --
 statically enabling the bind would give anything able to write
 /mnt/state/quadlet a root-capable container at the next reboot with no operator
 decision in the path. mosd's ContainerReconciler enables and starts it from the
 container.enabled setting, which is the runtime-scoped enablement every other
 mos-driven unit uses.
 .
 THE THREE DEPENDENCIES BELOW ARE WRITTEN OUT BECAUSE dpkg-shlibdeps CANNOT SEE
 THE REASON FOR THEM. Everything it can see -- libc6, libgcc-s1, libcap2 and
 libjson-c5 for crun, libsubid5 and libseccomp2 for podman, libglib2.0-0t64 for
 conmon -- is supplied by the shlibs substitution over the payload's ELF, with the
 version floors it derives from each library's symbols file, and is not restated
 here. A hand-written copy of one of those would be a version-less duplicate of a
 constraint the packer derives correctly.
 .
 nftables is reached by exec, not by linking. netavark 2.1.0 has three firewall
 drivers (Firewalld, Nftables, Fwnone); the iptables driver was removed in 2.x,
 nftables is the compile-time default, and netavark runs `nft` off PATH. Without
 it the first `podman run` fails with "netavark: nftables error: unable to
 execute nft", and no NEEDED-soname check can predict that.
 .
 libsystemd0 is reached by dlopen, not by linking, and is written out even though
 the shlibs substitution emits it as well. Those are two INDEPENDENT REASONS that
 happen to share one package name, and only one of them is derivable. The
 underivable one: go-systemd's sdjournal opens "libsystemd.so.0" by name at
 runtime, so it is absent from podman's NEEDED and ldd cannot see it, while
 containers.conf sets log_driver = "journald", so its absence loses container logs
 rather than failing loudly. The derivable one belongs to a DIFFERENT binary --
 conmon links libsystemd outright, and pkgs/podman/out-<arch>/NEEDED.txt
 records "conmon: libsystemd.so.0".
 .
 Dropping the written-out entry would therefore rest this package's correctness on
 an incidental property of conmon. If conmon ever stops linking libsystemd -- an
 upstream change or a build-flag change, neither of which this package controls --
 the substitution stops emitting it, nothing here declares it, and podman's
 journald logging fails the quiet way. One duplicated name in this field is the
 price of that not happening silently.
 .
 mos-system is depended on at the EXACT POOL VERSION, and this entry CHANGED ITS
 MIND. The dependency itself has not moved: mos-system owns the mounts this
 package's configuration names -- storage.conf puts the container graphroot on
 /mos/containers/storage, which is the MOS-owned namespace on DATA,
 and etc-containers-systemd.mount binds /mnt/state/quadlet, which needs
 mos-seed-state.service from the same package. That is why this package depends
 on it at all, and it is unchanged.
 .
 What changed is who builds mos-system. This entry was written UNVERSIONED
 deliberately, and the reason held at the time: no producer emitted mos-system,
 so the pool gate classified the reference EXTERNAL and only reported it, and
 pinning a package this repository did not build would have asserted a coupling
 that did not exist. That has been superseded -- rootfs/packages-src/system
 now produces mos-system from THIS commit, so the gate classifies the reference
 local-real and requires the exact pool version. The pool carries one version by
 rule, so the pin costs nothing inside it and stops an archive from resolving
 against a different pool generation; mos-apid already spells its mosd
 dependency the same way.
 .
 There is no dependency on mosd, mos-apid, mos-mqttd or mos-mqtt-broker. The
 engine is installed alongside them, not above them: it runs without any of them
 present, and mosd's use of it is mosd's dependency and not this one's.
